# PR and issue workflow

Before creating a pull request, open a tracking issue in the destination's native system (GitHub Issues for this repo). Link that issue through the work lifecycle and only close it after merge when pipelines are clean.

## Before the PR

1. Open an issue describing the problem, goal, and acceptance criteria.
2. Use the issue number in commits and the eventual PR.

Commit message example:

```
fix: pin go-api-signature to v0.3.12

omni/client v1.8.1 is incompatible with go-api-signature v0.3.13 (gopenpgp v2/v3).

Fixes #42
```

Use `Fixes #N`, `Closes #N`, or `Refs #N` consistently with the repo's linking conventions.

## While the PR is open

- Reference the issue in the PR title or description (e.g. `Fixes #42`).
- Update the issue as work progresses: scope changes, blockers, review feedback, and CI failures.
- Do not treat the PR description alone as the source of truth — keep the issue current.

## On merge

1. Confirm GitHub Actions (and any other required pipelines) completed successfully on the merged commit on `main`.
2. If pipelines fail on `main`, reopen or keep the issue open, fix forward, and re-verify.
3. Close the issue only after pipelines are green on the merged result.

## Agent checklist

- [ ] Issue opened before PR creation
- [ ] Commits reference the issue (`Fixes` / `Refs #N`)
- [ ] PR links to the issue
- [ ] Issue updated during review and CI iterations
- [ ] Post-merge pipeline status checked on `main`
- [ ] Issue closed only after clean pipeline runs
